A directory that will not list is no match, not a fatal error

`CaseInsensitiveAppendToPath` in `extern/Pomme/src/Files/HostVolume.cpp` answers one
question: is there a file here whose name matches apart from case? It answers it by
listing the parent directory, and it uses the throwing `fs::directory_iterator`, so a
listing that fails for any reason raises `filesystem_error` instead of answering.

On this console that is fatal. Bugdom reaches `$HOME/.config` during start-up, `getdents`
on it fails, libc++'s `directory_iterator` constructor throws, and nothing between there
and `main` catches it:

    [BUGD00001:cxx] std::terminate reached - an exception found no handler, or a handler threw
    [BUGD00001:cxx]   it was a std::exception:
    [BUGD00001:cxx] filesystem error: in directory_iterator::directory_iterator(...): unknown error ["/app0/.config"]

`main` does have `catch (std::exception&)` and `catch (...)`, so the throw is caught
eventually - but only after `Boot()` is abandoned, which ends the game before its first
frame.

The non-throwing overload is the right API for the question. "The listing failed" and
"nothing matched" are the same answer to this function's caller, which goes on to use the
naive concatenation either way. Nothing else changes: a directory that lists is walked
exactly as before.

Why the listing fails at all is a separate matter - `/app0` is the package mount and
`getdents` on it is not something this side can change - and it is the reason the question
is asked with the overload that can say no.

